WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.240
How do you prove a

2
00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:03.780
problem NP-complete? Show that it

3
00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:05.780
is in NP, then reduce

4
00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.620
a known NP-complete problem to

5
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.660
it in polynomial time. The

6
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.780
first half supplies verifiable witnesses,

7
00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.760
while the second transfers hardness

8
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.680
in the correct direction; neither

9
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:16.660
half can be omitted.

10
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:19.420
A language is NP-hard when

11
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:21.420
every NP language reduces to

12
00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.640
it. It is NP-complete only

13
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:24.660
when it is also in

14
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.480
NP, so NP-hard alone does

15
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.120
not imply an NP verifier

16
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.480
or even decidability. The known

17
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.320
SAT completeness theorem can be

18
00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.460
used as a starting point

19
00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.560
here; the next lessons explain

20
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.880
its Cook Levin proof.

21
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.980
Convert optimization MaxSAT into the

22
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.080
decision language asking whether at

23
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.480
least k clauses of a

24
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.180
given formula can be simultaneously

25
00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.660
satisfied. A truth assignment is

26
00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:51.480
a polynomial-size certificate. Evaluate each

27
00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.160
clause, count the true ones

28
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.520
and compare the count with

29
00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.820
k; checking the conjunction as

30
00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.320
a single Boolean would lose

31
00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.940
the requested threshold condition.

32
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.560
For hardness, transform a SAT

33
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.540
formula with m clauses into

34
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.140
the same formula with threshold

35
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.740
m. The SAT instance is

36
00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.000
satisfiable exactly when all m

37
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.340
clauses can be satisfied at

38
00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.520
once, exactly the target condition.

39
00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.760
The transformation just counts and

40
00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.280
writes the threshold, so it

41
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:18.820
is polynomial.

42
00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:20.980
Combining that mapping with the

43
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.580
assignment verifier proves decision MaxSAT

44
00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.060
NP-complete. To recover the optimum

45
00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.320
number of satisfiable clauses from

46
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.720
a decision solver, binary-search the

47
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:32.800
monotone threshold between zero and

48
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.520
m. This takes logarithmically many

49
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.760
decision calls, but finding the

50
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:39.500
optimum value is not yet

51
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:40.840
the same as printing an

52
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:41.860
optimal assignment.

53
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.800
DoubleSAT asks whether a formula

54
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.360
has at least two distinct

55
00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.680
satisfying assignments. Its NP certificate

56
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.760
is two full assignments, with

57
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.120
checks that they differ and

58
00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.500
both satisfy the formula. Listing

59
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.880
the same assignment twice does

60
00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.580
not meet the definition, even

61
00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.220
if the formula evaluates true

62
00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:00.920
both times.

63
00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.800
Reduce SAT to DoubleSAT by

64
00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:05.960
adjoining a tautological clause involving

65
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.000
a fresh variable z. If

66
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:09.940
the original formula is satisfiable,

67
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:11.840
keep its assignment and choose

68
00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.720
z either false or true,

69
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.420
giving two distinct extensions. If

70
00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.740
the original is unsatisfiable, adding

71
00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.320
a tautology cannot create a

72
00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:21.320
satisfying assignment.

73
00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.840
This target language uses general

74
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.320
conjunctive normal form, so the

75
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.000
new two-literal tautological clause is

76
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.360
allowed. Do not silently impose

77
00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.740
the exact-three restriction from three

78
00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:36.020
SAT on DoubleSAT. Valid output

79
00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:37.240
syntax is one of the

80
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.920
reduction’s obligations, not an afterthought.

81
00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.060
The hardness arrow must point

82
00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.520
from the known hard source

83
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.920
into the new target. Reducing

84
00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.480
the target to SAT gives

85
00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.220
an upper comparison and does

86
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.220
not establish the target NP-hard.

87
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.280
Transitivity explains why a known-complete

88
00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.800
source suffices: every NP language

89
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:58.360
reduces to it and then

90
00:02:58.360 --> 00:02:59.440
through your mapping to the

91
00:02:59.440 --> 00:02:59.860
target.

92
00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.780
If any NP-complete language has

93
00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:05.380
a polynomial decider, then every

94
00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:07.900
NP language does, implying P

95
00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:11.240
equals NP. Conversely, P equals

96
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.280
NP would put every NP-complete

97
00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.700
language in P. NP-completeness therefore

98
00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.300
does not prove an exponential

99
00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.860
lower bound or settle the

100
00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:20.320
unresolved equality question.

101
00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.120
Things to take note of.

102
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.840
Formalize the decision version, give

103
00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.520
a complete polynomial verifier, choose

104
00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.520
an independently known complete source

105
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.240
and prove its mapping into

106
00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.460
your target. Check both answer

107
00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.800
directions, output syntax and construction

108
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.660
cost; then keep optimization-value recovery

109
00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:40.620
separate from the completeness proof.
